 {:db/id "q-126287" :question/id #uuid "c9fca4a1-9999-5571-b7b3-a6f45eb203a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-126287" :question/problem "Which of the following are column vectors?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- $A$ is *not* a column vector. It has only one row (and $2$ columns), so it's a row vector.\n\n- $B$ is a column vector since it has only one column.\n\n- $C$ is *not* a column vector, since it has more than $1$ column (it has $2$ columns).\n\nTherefore, only $B$ is a column vector." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-126287/ma-source-field/a5b86bd6cfcbbeed7100dd3e857785f859566006cf7c25de68de67b06358cfbe"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
