 {:db/id "q-126838/ma-source-field/ce22a0bc590c8dcfb19f6eb0aee3c9bc8fb5a448584a0d438f5e5d19fbb79cd4" :answer-field/id #uuid "15cc7274-0fbf-5ba4-9530-45869135aca7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-126838/ma-source-field/ce22a0bc590c8dcfb19f6eb0aee3c9bc8fb5a448584a0d438f5e5d19fbb79cd4/eb85cf08e7711229547bc980a2937c7f9b94e18e81f7d410e29ba5d5c0877821" "q-126838/ma-source-field/ce22a0bc590c8dcfb19f6eb0aee3c9bc8fb5a448584a0d438f5e5d19fbb79cd4/bcccd548388eb8f94a8a828dc7d0d9fb215f845d7acc1a8e9d0d7ea88cae9a07" "q-126838/ma-source-field/ce22a0bc590c8dcfb19f6eb0aee3c9bc8fb5a448584a0d438f5e5d19fbb79cd4/3cd2a6a4155cb5a137eef8ab799fdcd9ef01ac81ca2e085f0796f488bbac67f8" "q-126838/ma-source-field/ce22a0bc590c8dcfb19f6eb0aee3c9bc8fb5a448584a0d438f5e5d19fbb79cd4/7057006c7f56f438b323cfc7db2bcbfabef737513340a4d19d8b32266be97742" "q-126838/ma-source-field/ce22a0bc590c8dcfb19f6eb0aee3c9bc8fb5a448584a0d438f5e5d19fbb79cd4/fddd5c79841c5440bcfa652eb285544dc44db35685a12725bfcb708f077053be"] :answer-field/correct "q-126838/ma-source-field/ce22a0bc590c8dcfb19f6eb0aee3c9bc8fb5a448584a0d438f5e5d19fbb79cd4/3cd2a6a4155cb5a137eef8ab799fdcd9ef01ac81ca2e085f0796f488bbac67f8" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-126838" :question/id #uuid "b063a87b-7453-5eed-82c3-d4c7e3cbe51f" :question/math-academy-id "q-126838" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ at the point where $t=\\pi ,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" :question/worked-solution "At the point where $t=\\pi ,$ the vector ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(\\pi )+u{r}^{′}(\\pi ),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point where $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨0,1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-126838/ma-source-field/ce22a0bc590c8dcfb19f6eb0aee3c9bc8fb5a448584a0d438f5e5d19fbb79cd4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
