 {:db/id "q-126874/ma-source-field/2289c29bca4d289252872ab689b6252333f170fdab2ceab56ad1c08056415a3b" :answer-field/id #uuid "5265398a-cfae-5735-aad4-474a4cb4c335" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-126874/ma-source-field/2289c29bca4d289252872ab689b6252333f170fdab2ceab56ad1c08056415a3b/bc799af8e53c0739140dad402171c83c59e994f530ff359a5f9683f32995c931" "q-126874/ma-source-field/2289c29bca4d289252872ab689b6252333f170fdab2ceab56ad1c08056415a3b/c232601054288fc6d53621aafc0b9f2cb31bffd3e7df589afe6b3f35c0f7ba19" "q-126874/ma-source-field/2289c29bca4d289252872ab689b6252333f170fdab2ceab56ad1c08056415a3b/cbfd2b39f693669c9c6d9bac9b9e54b113e25e0bbc185e2c1409c73d8346759f" "q-126874/ma-source-field/2289c29bca4d289252872ab689b6252333f170fdab2ceab56ad1c08056415a3b/6a04715d4b13527fbbb83dad17f1f0d74d048ac16e8dbd44aaa00e980b620a24" "q-126874/ma-source-field/2289c29bca4d289252872ab689b6252333f170fdab2ceab56ad1c08056415a3b/1a08162c562ec1931896a020e5565b2a6f3bbf7a8beba892d750fb1133db1af8"] :answer-field/correct "q-126874/ma-source-field/2289c29bca4d289252872ab689b6252333f170fdab2ceab56ad1c08056415a3b/c232601054288fc6d53621aafc0b9f2cb31bffd3e7df589afe6b3f35c0f7ba19" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-126874" :question/id #uuid "001d0357-fa46-5892-877e-9b6cc4712426" :question/math-academy-id "q-126874" :question/problem "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ about $x=\\pi $ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡3.$ Give your answer to $5$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\pi .$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi =0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =-1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi =0 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =(0)+(-1)(x-\\pi )+\\frac{(0)}{2!}(x-\\pi )^{2}+\\frac{(1)}{3!}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =-(x-\\pi )+\\frac{1}{6}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=3$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{sin}⁡3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3 & ≈-(3-\\pi )+\\frac{1}{6}(3-\\pi )^{3} \\\\  & =0.141\\,12,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto five decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-126874/ma-source-field/2289c29bca4d289252872ab689b6252333f170fdab2ceab56ad1c08056415a3b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
