 {:db/id "q-126877/ma-source-field/26538bc0230f24da49bb5582ffa4267e2b8fd3fcfbe990945e8703f72d6703af" :answer-field/id #uuid "4855ece8-350c-52ea-af76-5afe755310a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-126877/ma-source-field/26538bc0230f24da49bb5582ffa4267e2b8fd3fcfbe990945e8703f72d6703af/f9378424d1fb8ed709cf83b2f9b03ec28d57d5c704fc1aaeee2e8628ffdbdbdb" "q-126877/ma-source-field/26538bc0230f24da49bb5582ffa4267e2b8fd3fcfbe990945e8703f72d6703af/79a854fe7b3a86e2a5b3ba9ee6e3053f52b2d873a59eca3034b90616d4d0ea97" "q-126877/ma-source-field/26538bc0230f24da49bb5582ffa4267e2b8fd3fcfbe990945e8703f72d6703af/29427bd1b56828b1d2904e53f717ae4db1f18cf690df26c8431b1cc7d8f9adf0" "q-126877/ma-source-field/26538bc0230f24da49bb5582ffa4267e2b8fd3fcfbe990945e8703f72d6703af/a96814ed9c4a3fdd46acee1a8c3bf14a7a41aaa782c8803c793ac012efd03aac" "q-126877/ma-source-field/26538bc0230f24da49bb5582ffa4267e2b8fd3fcfbe990945e8703f72d6703af/d5d616dfff9b356a048a3bc19019f0875e57850733ce63894f310b3733271ceb"] :answer-field/correct "q-126877/ma-source-field/26538bc0230f24da49bb5582ffa4267e2b8fd3fcfbe990945e8703f72d6703af/79a854fe7b3a86e2a5b3ba9ee6e3053f52b2d873a59eca3034b90616d4d0ea97" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-126877" :question/id #uuid "9fcccafb-96d2-5248-a5bc-581af0a17196" :question/math-academy-id "q-126877" :question/problem "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}).$ Give your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =1+(2)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{(4)}{2!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) & ≈1+2(\\frac{\\pi }{5}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+2{(\\frac{\\pi }{5}-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =1+2(-\\frac{\\pi }{20})+2{(-\\frac{\\pi }{20})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{200} \\\\  & =0.735\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-126877/ma-source-field/26538bc0230f24da49bb5582ffa4267e2b8fd3fcfbe990945e8703f72d6703af"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
