 {:db/id "q-128480" :question/id #uuid "04472571-0a00-5fac-9502-9b8f8afd8c8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-128480" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nI. $D$ is a singular matrix\nII. $det(D)=1$\nIII. $det(D)=0$\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(4-15)+3(-2+9)+1(-5+6) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-11)+3\\cdot 7+1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-22+21+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(D)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-128480/ma-source-field/7fb2d0f2f6e655e2536f4e238f4f2d8d6dfc14abcf24f1e56096bb2e4628aa85"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
