 {:db/id "q-129197" :question/id #uuid "3b42698a-11e2-5e4e-8c95-f9d962f16e6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-129197" :question/problem "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{3z}{x+6y}?$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{3z}{x+6y} & =c \\\\ 3z & =c(x+6y) \\\\ 3z & =cx+6cy \\\\ z & =\\frac{c}{3}x+2cy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get a family of planes:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{c}{3}x+2cy,\\,c\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-129197/ma-source-field/3b4b4d92ec880e63d1b5f219b8ce61ecc02d0900e28ca55664123dc06b3a6fcd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
