 {:db/id "q-129198" :question/id #uuid "b82355ef-f6b0-5644-b274-2b1d50d4f10b" :question/math-academy-id "q-129198" :question/problem "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y+z)?$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y+z) & =c \\\\ x-2y+z & ={e}^{c}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the exponent ${e}^{c}$ is positive for all $-\\infty <c<\\infty .$ If we let ${e}^{c}={r}^{2},$ we get a family of planes:\n\n$$\nx-2y+z={r}^{2},\\,r\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-129198/ma-source-field/b9706b4bd73d2010ef818d33186937f9db87c801f09db8fcd8778d9b5923ac60"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
