 {:db/id "q-129213" :question/id #uuid "6061d8ea-d309-5b82-8a65-c5a4fc1b804c" :question/math-academy-id "q-129213" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -9\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $B$ are in row echelon form." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-129213/ma-source-field/ad157c00e52d62b7663728b1c450399c3283f46533f27b0493739ac85523620d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
