 {:db/id "q-129224/ma-source-field/1d4219174ab3a63051461d81fb0e08d29025232a5b6b41979d6b0fa0f5c12037" :answer-field/id #uuid "17d7ccaf-18fd-5fe2-bf5c-7ec52555ab3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-129224/ma-source-field/1d4219174ab3a63051461d81fb0e08d29025232a5b6b41979d6b0fa0f5c12037/eefa33de4685af6b07ddf8607ad9807e9342670e36d2dbe1f4a5b4876dc077d7" "q-129224/ma-source-field/1d4219174ab3a63051461d81fb0e08d29025232a5b6b41979d6b0fa0f5c12037/21a9c3d10b913ef40ac88b773ad113bae622e35ee423894258732fb1d8ff078b" "q-129224/ma-source-field/1d4219174ab3a63051461d81fb0e08d29025232a5b6b41979d6b0fa0f5c12037/86778a7c61f3118edf4ca5740841ef4b917716b96b5f7cef9a2d9900dff8ae89" "q-129224/ma-source-field/1d4219174ab3a63051461d81fb0e08d29025232a5b6b41979d6b0fa0f5c12037/7eeed55e4045d0d569350f75f04086486059021dea0afe123adb0db2eb3b7ba6" "q-129224/ma-source-field/1d4219174ab3a63051461d81fb0e08d29025232a5b6b41979d6b0fa0f5c12037/4d77c78d7238ba49f43fbe65d2ee5712c555bb5d8d7747685a0d83504f7a8a21"] :answer-field/correct "q-129224/ma-source-field/1d4219174ab3a63051461d81fb0e08d29025232a5b6b41979d6b0fa0f5c12037/7eeed55e4045d0d569350f75f04086486059021dea0afe123adb0db2eb3b7ba6" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-129224" :question/id #uuid "923148c0-0ecb-5f91-a025-b18771e50157" :question/math-academy-id "q-129224" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $2x-y-3z-8=0$ and $x+2y-3z-4=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n2x-y-3(0)-8=0\\,⟹\\,y=2x-8,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $y=2x-8$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2(2x-8)-0-4 & =0 \\\\ x+4x-16-4 & =0 \\\\ 5x & =20 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2(4)-8=0$ and $a=⟨4,0,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y-3z-8=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,-1,-3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-3z-4=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨2,-1,-3⟩\\times ⟨1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =9i+3j+5k \\\\  & =⟨9,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨4,0,0⟩+t⟨9,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=4+0=4,$ and $b=⟨9,3,5⟩.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-129224/ma-source-field/1d4219174ab3a63051461d81fb0e08d29025232a5b6b41979d6b0fa0f5c12037"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
