 {:db/id "q-130217/ma-source-field/5c0f2cd612f45df214acea16a9c35f92fff0dc62cd36909dddce32cdd324e620" :answer-field/id #uuid "31717cb2-d9d0-54f4-b700-91183260eebd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-130217/ma-source-field/5c0f2cd612f45df214acea16a9c35f92fff0dc62cd36909dddce32cdd324e620/f421030cb462aa616bae5fc2f08c375a5693fb03530ada533356230ff49fde05" "q-130217/ma-source-field/5c0f2cd612f45df214acea16a9c35f92fff0dc62cd36909dddce32cdd324e620/c996b9113bb1a71c7e803b02f6e5820dcc204fc407a64a4581f5251bd2e7e481" "q-130217/ma-source-field/5c0f2cd612f45df214acea16a9c35f92fff0dc62cd36909dddce32cdd324e620/7f1f41c6a09c92f948d91b67a83f53722f4fa81ad1e1c59ff9771d09615ae187" "q-130217/ma-source-field/5c0f2cd612f45df214acea16a9c35f92fff0dc62cd36909dddce32cdd324e620/6c24b52ce73794803be5eec4914891a0c0af37a54e52dd150a69de24ea3ae00f" "q-130217/ma-source-field/5c0f2cd612f45df214acea16a9c35f92fff0dc62cd36909dddce32cdd324e620/c902e9cdb1317f7226da5493d556cf04b400e17f1f8619cb55f7c12a0cc2a2e6"] :answer-field/correct "q-130217/ma-source-field/5c0f2cd612f45df214acea16a9c35f92fff0dc62cd36909dddce32cdd324e620/7f1f41c6a09c92f948d91b67a83f53722f4fa81ad1e1c59ff9771d09615ae187" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-130217" :question/id #uuid "6851bbc7-80d6-5dc9-8a00-f559624fa12f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130217" :question/problem "At which points on the curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2y-2x$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $-i+j?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2x-2y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2y-2)\\,i-(2x+2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $-i+j,$ we must have $t\\cdot (-i+j)=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot (-i+j) & =0 \\\\ ((2y-2)\\,i-(2x+2)\\,j)\\cdot (-i+j) & =0 \\\\ -(2y-2)-(2x+2) & =0 \\\\ -2y-2x & =0 \\\\ y & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+(-x)^{2} & =2(-x)-2x \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =-4x \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+4x & =0 \\\\ 2x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- For $x=-2,$ we get $y=2,$ and the corresponding point is $(-2,2).$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=0,$ and the corresponding point is $(0,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-130217/ma-source-field/5c0f2cd612f45df214acea16a9c35f92fff0dc62cd36909dddce32cdd324e620"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
